```java
// The class — a blueprint, no memory allocated here yet
class Student {
    String name;
    int marks;

    void printGrade() {
        System.out.println(name + ": " + (marks >= 50 ? "Pass" : "Fail"));
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Two objects from the same class — fully independent
        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.name = "Alice";
        s1.marks = 95;

        Student s2 = new Student();
        s2.name = "Bob";
        s2.marks = 42;

        s1.printGrade();  // Alice: Pass
        s2.printGrade();  // Bob: Fail

        // Proof of independence
        s1.marks = 60;
        System.out.println(s2.marks); // 42 — unaffected
    }
}
```
Same class, two objects, completely independent data.

This short program proves the order of events. Before the constructor sets anything, the fields already hold their defaults — and two references to the same `new` object see each other's changes.

```java
public class NewKeywordDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Step 1+2 already done before this line finishes:
        // heap space reserved, fields default-filled (name=null, marks=0).
        // Step 3: constructor sets real values. Step 4: reference assigned in s1.
        Student s1 = new Student("Alice", 95);

        // A second reference to the SAME heap object — no new object created
        Student s2 = s1;
        s2.marks = 100;
        System.out.println(s1.marks); // 100 — s1 and s2 point to one object

        // Setting s1 = null only drops the reference; the object still existed
        s1 = null;
        System.out.println(s2.marks); // 100 — object survives via s2
    }
}

class Student {
    String name;
    int marks; // default-filled to 0 before the constructor runs

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }
}
```
One `new` call, one heap object — and `null`-ing one reference never touches the data another reference still holds.

